6. Redesign this code to use proper polymorphism instead of instanceof chains:
class NotificationSender {
    public void send(Object notification) {
        if (notification instanceof EmailNotif) {
            EmailNotif e = (EmailNotif) notification;
            System.out.println("Sending email to: " + e.getEmailAddress());
        } else if (notification instanceof SmsNotif) {
            SmsNotif s = (SmsNotif) notification;
            System.out.println("Sending SMS to: " + s.getPhoneNumber());
        } else if (notification instanceof PushNotif) {
            System.out.println("Sending push notification");
        }
    }
}

items.a:
Create an interface or abstract class with a send() method, and let each notification type implement it:

interface Notification {
    void send();
}
class EmailNotif implements Notification {
    private String email;
    @Override
    public void send() { System.out.println("Sending email to: " + email); }
}
class SmsNotif implements Notification {
    private String phone;
    @Override
    public void send() { System.out.println("Sending SMS to: " + phone); }
}
class PushNotif implements Notification {
    @Override
    public void send() { System.out.println("Sending push notification"); }
}
class NotificationSender {
    public void send(Notification n) { n.send(); } // Polymorphic — no instanceof!
}

Benefits: (1) Adding a new notification type requires zero changes to NotificationSender. (2) No ClassCastException risk. (3) Follows Open/Closed Principle. (4) Code is shorter and more readable.

